A video sink that has no decoder of the device (westerosrawsink) writes each
frame through the CPU's memory into two buffers of the DRM device (brightness,
colour) and hands them to Westeros, which makes an image of each buffer for
OpenGL ES. With virtio-gpu's virgl (the host's GPU draws for the VM) such an
image is the host's texture of the buffer: what the guest wrote is never
taken to it, and it has the format the buffer was made with (32 bits per
pixel), not the one asked for. The video was black, and the user interface
did not come back after it.

Where the renderer is virgl, the two planes are now copied into textures of
one and two bytes per pixel, which the shader for two planes reads as it
reads the images. WESTEROS_RENDER_COPY_VIDEO in the environment asks for the
copy with any renderer (1) or with none (0).

